  "live_records": {
    "q-125044": {
      "dom_id": "step-q125044",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{x-y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-2,1)$ to the point $B(1,-1)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(-2+3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j.$*",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=-2\\,i+j$ and $b=i-j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+j)+t[(i-j)-(-2\\,i+j)] \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+j)+t(3\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(-2+3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=-2+3t,\\,y=1-2t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x+y)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{x-y}\\,j \\\\  & =(-2+3t+1-2t)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{-2+3t-(1-2t)}\\,j \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{5t-3}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2+3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((t-1)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{5t-2}\\,j)\\cdot (3\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(t-1)^{2}\\cdot 3+{e}^{5t-3}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3(t-1)^{2}-2{e}^{5t-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3(t-1)^{2}-2{e}^{5t-3}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
