    "q-125053": {
      "dom_id": "step-q125053",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(4,2).$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are \n$a=2\\,i$ and $b=4\\,i+2\\,j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(2\\,i)+t[(4\\,i+2\\,j)-(2\\,i)] \\\\  & =(2\\,i)+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t+2)\\,i+2t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2t+2\\,y=2t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\,i+{e}^{2t+2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{2t}\\,i+{e}^{2t+2}\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\cdot 2+{e}^{2t+2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(1+{e}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{2t}(1+{e}^{2})\\,dt \\\\  & =(1+{e}^{2}){e}^{2t}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1+{e}^{2})({e}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{4}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
