    "q-124703": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124703",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $A(1,1)$ to the point $B(2,7)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=t\\,i+(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j.$*",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. This is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j,\\,1\\le t\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-1)j \\\\  & =i+4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,j)\\cdot (i+4t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{t}^{2}-1)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
