    "q-125065": {
      "dom_id": "step-q125065",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+2y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-x=0$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,e).$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j,\\,1\\le t\\le e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+2y\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+2t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}+2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡t}{2}+{t}^{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}+{e}^{2})-(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
