    "q-125027": {
      "dom_id": "step-q125027",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=3{x}^{2}+1$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,4).$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Since $y$ is the subject of the equation, a parametrization is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =t\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}+1)j \\\\  & =i+6t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\,j)\\cdot (i+6t\\,j) \\\\  & =t\\cdot 1+(3{t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 6t \\\\  & =t+18{t}^{3}+6t \\\\  & =7t+18{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(7t+18{t}^{3})\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{7(1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9(1)^{4}}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
