    },
    "q-125171": {
      "dom_id": "step-q125171",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14222363/assets/f7bacafcbe893c0d21ea45a916190a49aa3a3532834cff4704f707c100bf8e6a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=3y\\,i+2x\\,j$ and $C$ is the the arc of the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3$ respectively, traversed anticlockwise from $A(0,3)$ to $B(-1,0)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.$*",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the parametrization of an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3$ respectively. It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =3y\\,i+2x\\,j \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-9{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =6{cos}^{2}⁡t-9{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(6{cos}^{2}⁡t-9{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
