    "q-125234": {
      "dom_id": "step-q125234",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin traversed counterclockwise from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-3,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. This curve can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+27{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(3{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(3{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(3{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & =9{[{sin}^{3}⁡t-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{2}]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =9[0-(1-\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
