      "source_example_id": "e-9531",
      "knowledge_point": "Line Integrals Over Circles and Ellipses",
      "kp_id": "e-9531"
    },
    "q-39427": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39427",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14222363/assets/c5e026312ab8a2ccfc61a37246c954b56550316661f158743cc2c46411a97f53.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(y-x)\\,i-x\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=2$  traversed counterclockwise from the point $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ to the point $(0,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Notice that we can write its equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our curve is a part-ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$ respectively. This curve can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(y-x)\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\sqrt{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-\\sqrt{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\sqrt{2}t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}]-[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}]-[-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-39427-a",
