    "q-39341": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39341",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14222363/assets/4807f92defb4202359a32ca046073cd138db396a0975164e42b96126ac0cc591.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i+{e}^{-2y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin traversed counterclockwise from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(0,1)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.$*",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a part-circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. This curve can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={e}^{x}\\,i+{e}^{-2y}\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
